  So the realising set of 1^u q 1^v is  { x : x+1 = 2^{u+1} A,  A odd,
  A == 3^{-(u+1)}(1-2^{q-1}) mod 2^{q+v} }, and
        2^{u+1} - 1  <=  r(1^u q 1^v)  <  2^{u+q+v+2} .
  The two run costs MULTIPLY and there is no cross term: the first run
  contributes the factor 2^{u+1} and the second contributes a residue class
  mod 2^{q+v}.  ANSWER TO THE PRIMARY QUESTION: NO extra joint cost.

  When is the lower bound 2^{u+1}-1 attained?  Exactly when A_min = 1, i.e.
  3^{u+1} == 1 - 2^{q-1} mod 2^{q+v}, i.e. when x = 2^{u+1}-1 is itself the
  realiser -- the Mersenne/all-ones-prefix seeds, for which q and v are
